{"kidney":{"id":"9839f8e9-8b82-4bb8-93c9-686aa778dee7","word":"kidney","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"an organ in the body","tags":["masculine"],"word":"rim"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"food","tags":["masculine"],"word":"rim"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The liver and the kidneys of animals have long been gastronomically popular for their aphrodisiac properties.","ref":"1961, Harry E. Wedeck, Dictionary of Aphrodisiacs, New York: The Citadel Press, page 124:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[18,25]],"id":"6cdf14c3-df35-486f-a9a3-be6a76a02beb"},{"text":"An artificial kidney these days still means a refrigerator-sized dialysis machine. Such devices mimic the way real kidneys cleanse blood and eject impurities and surplus water as urine.","ref":"2013 June 1, “A better waterworks”, in The Economist, volume 407, number 8838, page 5 (Technology Quarterly):","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[14,20],[115,121],[115,122]],"id":"c3ec9361-97c7-4eef-89d6-b7afb60ee18d"}],"wikidata":["Q9377"],"senseid":["en:Q9377"],"links":[["organ","organ"],["urine","urine"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["An organ in the body that filters the blood, producing urine."],"id":"35748d96-edb4-4553-9d0e-7e84177265cd"},{"links":[["food","food"]],"glosses":["This organ (of an animal) cooked as food."],"id":"1ccdfbdf-3112-4635-a8e7-8ded11694bd0"},{"examples":[{"text":"[…] think of that, – a man of my kidney, – think of that, […]","ref":"c. 1597 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Merry Wiues of Windsor”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act V, (please specify the scene number in lowercase Roman numerals)]:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[33,39]],"id":"d64df899-6559-43c0-920d-ffc81aad743c"},{"text":"Millions in the World of this Man's Kidney","ref":"1692, Roger L’Estrange, “ (please specify the fable number.) (please specify the name of the fable.)”, in Fables, of Æsop and Other Eminent Mythologists: […], London: […] R[ichard] Sare, […], →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[36,42]],"id":"dd3ea1b0-87c6-49dd-b706-26e6b7a376ea"},{"text":"30th June, 1788, Robert Burns, letter to Mr Robert Ainslie\nYour poets, spendthrifts, and other fools of that kidney, pretend, forsooth, to crack their jokes on prudence.","id":"a05f5fa0-0f8f-429f-a08f-42f6f09aaa7b"},{"text":"I shall not want Honour in Heaven\n For I shall meet Sir Philip Sidney\nAnd have talk with Coriolanus\n And other heroes of that kidney.","ref":"1920, T.S. Eliot, A Cooking Egg:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[126,132]],"id":"e5e7f43c-8032-4d30-b1af-47dc0b9d0851"}],"categories":["English dated terms","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["Constitution","constitution"],["temperament","temperament"],["nature","nature"],["type","type"],["character","character"],["disposition","disposition"]],"raw_glosses":["(figuratively, dated) Constitution, temperament, nature, type, character, disposition. (usually used of people)"],"glosses":["Constitution, temperament, nature, type, character, disposition. (usually used of people)"],"tags":["dated","figuratively"],"id":"28887530-48bb-40a7-b313-fe3b76e9edc4"},{"examples":[{"text":"I once more desire my readers to consider that as I cannot keep an ingenious man to go daily to Will's under twopence each day merely for his charges, to White's under sixpence, nor to the Grecian without allowing him some plain Spanish, to be as able as others at the learned table; and that a good observer cannot speak with even Kidney at St. James's without clean linen; […]","ref":"1709, Richard Steele, The Tatler, volume 1:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[332,338]],"id":"bca88d69-6579-4c1b-9edf-04f74f135c90"}],"categories":["English slang","English terms with obsolete senses","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["waiter","waiter"]],"raw_glosses":["(obsolete, slang) A waiter."],"glosses":["A waiter."],"tags":["obsolete","slang"],"id":"a4aa8ee0-cf79-407d-ae04-5e8467fb4dcf"}],"related":[{"word":"renal"},{"word":"suprarenal"}],"synonyms":[{"word":"rein"},{"word":"nephros"},{"word":"ren"}]},"kid":{"id":"f0b09b03-65d7-4045-ab36-3f681fac27b1","word":"kid","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"mock playfully","word":"enganar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"mock playfully","word":"caçoar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to joke","word":"brincar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to joke","word":"zoar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"Are you kidding me?","bold_text_offsets":[[8,15]],"type":"example","id":"de297fae-126a-459e-8470-400a2a89ceeb"},{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"I kid you not!","bold_text_offsets":[[2,5]],"type":"example","id":"9d4a8612-a4ac-4405-a544-69ab59465445"},{"text":"\"They are all very suspicious about the wording. I am always thinking up new ways of kidding them.\"","ref":"1965, James Holledge, What Makes a Call Girl?, London: Horwitz Publications, page 76:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[85,92]],"id":"98731015-3bf4-4c2c-864f-78ff3659405a"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English terms with collocations","English terms with quotations","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["dupe","dupe"],["deceive","deceive"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, colloquial, usually present participle) To dupe or deceive."],"glosses":["To dupe or deceive."],"tags":["colloquial","participle","present","transitive","usually"],"id":"8e905475-b007-4249-bc48-18282cab84d2"},{"examples":[{"text":"Stop kidding me! It's not funny.","bold_text_offsets":[[5,12]],"type":"example","id":"1fd5bde5-74e2-4433-850e-c5dfa81391ce"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English terms with collocations","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["dupe","dupe"],["deceive","deceive"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, colloquial, usually present participle) To dupe or deceive.","(transitive, colloquial, usually present participle) To deceive or dupe as a joke."],"glosses":["To dupe or deceive.","To deceive or dupe as a joke."],"tags":["colloquial","participle","present","transitive","usually"],"id":"c33a6788-563d-450b-bb40-4bd1da88ad0f"},{"examples":[{"text":"You're kidding yourself if you think you can be a rockstar.","bold_text_offsets":[[7,14]],"type":"example","id":"d513a46f-78a9-42f8-8e8a-8d4d18d10b8f"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English reflexive verbs","English terms with collocations","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["dupe","dupe"],["deceive","deceive"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, colloquial, usually present participle) To dupe or deceive.","(reflexive, usually present participle) To deceive oneself by having unrealistic expectations."],"glosses":["To dupe or deceive.","To deceive oneself by having unrealistic expectations."],"tags":["colloquial","participle","present","reflexive","transitive","usually"],"id":"306f3f5d-9782-49be-a6f0-b1ce8b23dbc5"},{"examples":[{"text":"They were always kidding her for her stutter.","bold_text_offsets":[[17,24]],"type":"example","id":"b84ec8b0-a932-47a4-96e3-89f72c114491"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["playful","playful"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, colloquial, usually present participle) To mock or make a fool of (someone) in a playful way."],"glosses":["To mock or make a fool of (someone) in a playful way."],"tags":["colloquial","participle","present","transitive","usually"],"id":"e827442f-3136-49ac-b41f-655d75e9c7bd"},{"examples":[{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"You must be kidding!","bold_text_offsets":[[12,19]],"type":"example","id":"6755ab2a-63e1-47cb-b083-92c5a9fe9381"},{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"I'm only kidding!","bold_text_offsets":[[9,16]],"type":"example","id":"ad08da46-3a07-4947-8835-e87d3dfc1eaf"},{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"No kidding!","bold_text_offsets":[[3,10]],"type":"example","id":"5bec60bd-12fd-4f63-9a30-caff2a3d728d"},{"text":"\"Blind!\" exclaimed the old fellow, sitting up in startled wonderment. \"You mean it, sir? You walk all right and you look at me as if you saw me. You're kidding surely.\"","ref":"1914, Ernest Bramah, Max Carrados:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[152,159]],"id":"db3dbff0-7a1a-4679-b4a7-ef2ced0732e9"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English intransitive verbs","English terms with collocations","English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"links":[["joke","joke"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive, colloquial, usually present participle) To joke."],"glosses":["To joke."],"tags":["colloquial","intransitive","participle","present","usually"],"id":"8c345ba6-df95-42c1-aacb-26ddf6c5f8b5"},{"examples":[{"text":"That nanny over there with the white tail has kidded every year for the last five years.","bold_text_offsets":[[46,52]],"type":"example","id":"5d99d1e5-3baa-4239-9b13-b6a405304f5c"},{"text":"\"They can kid twice a year if things are right, and they often throw twins and triplets.\"","ref":"2008, Monte Dwyer, Red In The Centre: The Australian Bush Through Urban Eyes, Monyer Pty Ltd, page 200:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[10,13]],"id":"efb4f693-9b85-4bf6-8c13-0fa6854d0562"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"links":[["give birth","give birth"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive) Of a goat: to give birth."],"glosses":["Of a goat: to give birth."],"tags":["intransitive"],"id":"a8e0410e-9563-4189-9ca8-27c1b92de00e"}],"related":[{"word":"kid on"},{"word":"suede"}],"synonyms":[]},